Module data licence
===================

The jack data in this directory is licensed under a Creative Commons
Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY 4.0). This is NOT the MIT licence
that covers the rest of this repository.

    Copyright (c) 2026 Gavin Sade and contributors
    https://github.com/ubertv/patching

Full legal code: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode
Human-readable summary: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/


Why this directory is licensed differently
------------------------------------------

It is a database, not software, and it contains three different kinds of thing.

Facts. Where a socket sits on a panel and what is printed next to it are facts
about physical objects. Nobody can own those, here or anywhere.

Prose. The normalling notes were written by hand, one module at a time. That is
original expression and it is covered by copyright.

The compilation. Assembling and verifying it took real work. In the EU and UK
that investment is protected by a sui generis database right, which exists
separately from copyright.

MIT is a software licence. It grants permissions over "the Software" and never
mentions database rights, so applying it here would leave anyone in a
database-right jurisdiction guessing about what they were allowed to do with the
compilation. CC BY 4.0 covers all three cases, and version 4.0 specifically
because it is the first CC version to pull sui generis database rights
explicitly into scope, at Section 4 of the legal code.


Using it
--------

Keep the copyright line above, state that the data is CC BY 4.0, link back, and
say if you changed anything. A credit line in an about box, a readme or a
footnote is enough. You do not need to cite individual jacks.

You can use it commercially. You can fold it into a project under any licence,
including a closed one. You can correct it, extend it, and redistribute it.


Contributing
------------

Contributions to this directory are accepted under the same licence, CC BY 4.0.
There is no agreement to sign. Opening a pull request is taken as offering your
contribution on those terms.

What matters more than the licence is provenance. Say where each jack came from:
which manual, which panel photograph, which module in front of you. Anything
the source could not settle goes in the module's "note". The value of this data
is that it can be trusted, and that only survives if every addition can be
traced back to something.


Material that is not covered
----------------------------

Anything added later under someone else's terms carries those terms, not these,
and will be marked where it sits. Panel photographs are the likely case: for
example, Mutable Instruments published their hardware files under CC BY-SA 3.0,
so any artwork derived from those stays CC BY-SA 3.0 with attribution to them.

Manufacturer names and module names are trademarks of their owners. Naming a
module in order to document it is nominative use and is not a claim on the mark.
